WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.440
In this video, you stop, start, restart, pause, and unpause a virtual machine from the Virtual Machines list, and see how its state changes.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:18.140
The virtual machine demo-power is running. Its power actions are in the action menu at the end of the row.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.870
Open the action menu.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.370
Select Stop.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:29.900
Confirm with Apply. The guest operating system receives a shutdown request.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:37.700
The virtual machine is now Off. It keeps its disks and configuration but uses no CPU or memory.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.510
To turn it back on, open the action menu

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:45.840
and select Start.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.880
The virtual machine is running again.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.770
Restart stops and starts the virtual machine in one step.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.400
Select Restart,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.750
and confirm with Apply.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.930
After a short stop, the virtual machine is running again.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:10.400
Pause freezes the virtual machine in memory without shutting it down.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.950
Select Pause.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.630
Confirm with Apply.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:20.980
The state changes to Paused. The guest stops running but keeps its memory.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.900
Open the action menu again

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.620
and select Unpause.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:32.260
The virtual machine continues exactly where it stopped.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:39.106
You can now control the power state of any virtual machine. Next, you use a cloud configuration template.
